  "subtitle": "An inflatable humanoid punching bag you physically fought to control a Famicom boxing game",
  "why_scout_cares": "In 1987, Konami shipped an inflatable, life-sized boxer with 11 internal pressure sensors that plugged into the Nintendo Famicom. You stood on a plastic mat, put on knitted gloves, and actually punched the bag—and your on-screen fighter threw the corresponding punches. It was full-body motion gaming nearly two decades before the Wii, packaged in a ¥7,980 box so enormous that Japanese retailers struggled to shelve it. The sensors were unreliable, the bag kept falling over, and it required inflation plus two liters of water ballast before every session. It sold terribly and was forgotten. That is exactly why it belongs here: it is what human-computer interaction looked like when nobody yet knew what the category was supposed to be.",
